import {texEq,inlineMath} from './math.js';
import {table,cite} from './topic-tools.js';
export function spectralTransfer(){return `<h3 id="functional-scaling">Follow the effect of an update through the layer</h3><p>For a row-vector input x and a weight matrix W, the linear output is y = xW. Fan-in initialization can keep output variance bounded at initialization. It does not determine the effect of a learned update, because the gradient can be correlated with x.</p>
${texEq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}W_{ij}&\sim\mathcal N\!\left(0,\frac{1}{d_{\mathrm{in}}}\right)\\\Delta y&=x\Delta W\\\|\Delta y\|_2&\le\|x\|_2\,\|\Delta W\|_2\end{aligned}`,'The first line is a common fan-in initialization, not a complete μP prescription. The final matrix norm is spectral norm. The bound holds for the linear layer at a fixed input.')}
<p>Small entry-wise changes can still combine into a large feature change. This is why keeping a weight norm or one raw learning rate constant is insufficient for width transfer. For a nonlinear layer ${inlineMath(String.raw`h_{\ell+1}=\phi(h_\ell W_\ell)`)}, its Jacobian J describes local input-to-output sensitivity. Forward propagation, backward propagation and update-induced changes must all be considered.</p><p>Across several layers, sensitivities compose as Jacobian products. Large operator norms can amplify perturbations; small singular directions can suppress them. One layer's spectral norm alone does not certify stable gradients through the whole model. μP supplies coordinated scaling rules, not a universal requirement that every Jacobian be orthogonal.</p><p>The reference Transformer recipe also changes attention-score scaling from ${inlineMath(String.raw`1/\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{head}}}`)} to a multiple of ${inlineMath(String.raw`1/d_{\mathrm{head}}`)}. Its example uses ${inlineMath(String.raw`8/d_{\mathrm{head}}`)}, which matches the usual scale when the head width is 64. This belongs to that parametrization; do not independently replace attention scaling in an arbitrary pretrained model. ${cite('https://github.com/microsoft/mup#basic-usage','Reference μP setup')}.</p>
<p>Use coordinate checks to compare activation magnitudes over several widths and learning rates. Then inspect actual update-induced feature changes and validate training behavior. A failed check is a reason to inspect initialization, readout, head scaling and optimizer groups—not simply lower every learning rate.</p><p>${cite('https://kexue.fm/archives/10766','μP width-transfer discussion')} · ${cite('https://kexue.fm/archives/10795','Spectral-scaling discussion')} · ${cite('https://kexue.fm/archives/11304','Further model-scaling discussion')} · ${cite('https://kexue.fm/archives/11260','Learning rate and batch foundations')}.</p>`;}
export function gradientDiagnostics(link){return `<h3 id="gradient-diagnostics">Read the gradient and the update separately</h3>
${texEq(String.raw`r_{\mathrm{grad}}=\frac{\|g\|_2}{\|\theta\|_2},\qquad r_{\mathrm{update}}=\frac{\|\Delta\theta\|_2}{\|\theta\|_2}`,'A zero parameter norm makes these ratios not defined. Log an absolute norm or specify an epsilon policy. The gradient ratio becomes an SGD update ratio only after accounting for learning rate; adaptive optimizers need the actual update.')}
${table('Diagnostics are clues, not certificates of model quality',['Observation','Check next'],[['Rare norm spikes','Identify the batch, reduction scale, precision and corresponding loss/update'],['Tiny early-layer gradients','Inspect saturation, masks, disconnected paths and layerwise gradient flow'],['Large head gradients, small backbone gradients','Check frozen parameters, intended adaptation targets and functional changes'],['Clipping on most steps','Inspect the raw distribution and threshold; frequent clipping can be intentional'],['Smooth norms but poor validation','Check data, objective and generalization; stable optimization is not model quality']])}
<p>Record raw and clipped norms together. Inspect trends at matched loss reductions and batch definitions. Norms need not decrease monotonically; changing the schedule, data or parameterization changes their interpretation. A large norm is not automatically harmful, and a small gradient does not imply a small Adam update.</p>
<pre><code># After correctly weighted gradient accumulation; scaler is an AMP GradScaler.
scaler.unscale_(optimizer)
raw_norm = torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(
    model.parameters(), max_norm=threshold, error_if_nonfinite=True
)
scaler.step(optimizer)
scaler.update()
optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)</code></pre>
<p>The function returns the total norm before clipping. This sketch assumes nonsharded gradients and an existing AMP training loop. Distributed/sharded training needs its framework's logical global-norm operation. Compare actual parameter values before and after the optimizer step to measure update scale. ${cite('https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/2.14/generated/torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_.html','PyTorch clipping contract')}.</p><p><a href="${link('training/stability')}#response">Training stability and run diagnosis →</a> · <a href="${link('math/calculus')}">Gradient and curvature geometry →</a></p>`;}
